    "leveling.intro_body": "Across every project, find where a crew or machine is booked beyond its capacity, then review concrete suggestions for which booking to shift or spread to bring it back in line. The grid is capacity-aware, so a resource with no declared capacity reads neutral rather than a false overload, and nothing moves until you confirm it. Suggestions and loads are computed from confirmed assignments against each resource capacity.",
    "leveling.intro_more": "The heatmap colours each cell against the resource capacity, not a flat ceiling: green well within capacity, amber as it approaches the limit, red once it is exceeded, and slate when the resource has no capacity set so an overload cannot honestly be claimed. A red ring marks a period where two or more projects compete for the same resource. Click any resource row to open the drawer.\n\nThe drawer is where leveling happens:\n\n- **Leveling suggestions** propose either a Shift (move a booking to an adjacent period) or a Spread (reduce a period and redistribute), each with a plain-language rationale, the project affected and the amount it is over by.\n- **Bookings by period** breaks every bucket down project by project so you can see exactly what is driving the overload.\n\nThe summary chips count resources booked, resources over capacity, resources with unknown capacity, and total suggestions, so you know the size of the problem before you dig in. Loads and suggestions read from confirmed assignments in the Resources module.\n\n**Getting the most out of it:** Set a real capacity on every resource first, an unknown-capacity row can never be levelled. Work the red rows from the most over-allocated down, and treat the suggestions as a starting point you confirm and apply in Resources, since this view shows what to do without moving anything on its own.",
    "about.book_p10": "The construction industry in the digital age - opportunities & challenges",
